<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raja Kecil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1746">1746</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raja Kecil was a Minangkabau-backed adventurer who claimed to be the posthumous son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-ii-of-johor">Mahmud Shah II of Johor</a>. In 1718 he seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> throne, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> forces drove him out in 1722. He then founded the Siak Sultanate on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>'s east coast, which became a leading Malay power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raja-kecil.jpg" alt="Raja Kecil" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris Est 13 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jirat_Makam_Marhum_Buantan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raja Kecil's origins are obscure and his date of birth cannot be established. Local legend held that he was the son of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-ii-of-johor">Mahmud Shah II of Johor</a>, conceived under supernatural circumstances on the eve of the sultan's murder in 1699, and that courtiers sent the child, whose name means little king, to the Minangkabau Highlands of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> under the protection of the ruling queen. Since he already had grown children in 1718, the story points instead to a Minangkabau origin.</p><p>The myth served him well. It let him appeal to the many Minangkabau migrants of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and to beliefs in the sanctity of the Johor-Malacca-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> line of kings, and it later linked the Siak state to the Malacca Sultanate. In the mid-1710s, then known as Tuan Bujang, he came to the port of Bengkalis and gathered merchants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> who resented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>'s control of their trade, and agreed plans with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> warriors Daeng Parani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daeng-chelak">Daeng Chelak</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Breaking that agreement, he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> without the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> in February 1718 and took its capital on 1 March, deposing the former Bendahara who ruled as Sultan Abdul Jalil Shah IV. He then withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, made Abdul Jalil Bendahara again and married his daughter Tengku Kamariah. Four unsettled years followed, as he moved between Sumatra and Johor and struggled to keep the loyalty of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a> followers. He finally ordered the Bendahara's assassination, and the dead man's sons agreed to share power with Bugis mercenaries in return for protection.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> pressure and without the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a>, he gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, which passed to Sultan Sulaiman under Bugis leaders such as Daeng Marewah. In 1722 he founded the Siak Sultanate at Buantan, ruling as Sultan Abdul Jalil Rahmat Shah. For another decade he fought the Bugis and raided Johor, while building a state on the Siak River that controlled trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and the Minangkabau interior.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to traditional texts, the death of Tengku Kamariah in the late 1730s plunged him into incapacitating melancholia. He let his council, the Four Penghulu, choose his successor. They chose his son Raja Mahmud, who ruled for at least five years before becoming Sultan Muhammad Abdul Jalil Jalaluddin Shah on his father's death in 1746; another son, Raja Alam, gathered followers to challenge him, a rivalry that troubled Siak for decades. Raja Kecil was buried at Buantan as Marhum Buantan, and his grave near Siak Sri Indrapura is visited as a holy site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raja Kecil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1718 <b>Raja Kecil</b> of Siak seized the throne, and in 1722 Bugis warriors expelled him and gained great power at court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Raja Kecil</b>, a Minangkabau-backed claimant who said he was the posthumous son of Sultan Mahmud II of Johor, seized the Johor Sultanate in 1718.</blockquote><a href="/state/siak-sultanate">Siak Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
